    <div class="eyebrow">Case Study &middot; Customer &amp; Growth</div>
    <h1>Customer Profitability and Commercial Repricing</h1>
    <div class="cs-meta">
      <div class="cs-meta-item"><div class="cs-meta-label">Industry</div><div class="cs-meta-value">Financial Services &amp; Professional Services</div></div>
      <div class="cs-meta-item"><div class="cs-meta-label">Area</div><div class="cs-meta-value">Customer Analytics, Commercial Strategy</div></div>
      <div class="cs-meta-item"><div class="cs-meta-label">Content</div><div class="cs-meta-value">Illustrative Case Study</div></div>
    </div>
    <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1441986300917-64674bd600d8?auto=format&fit=crop&q=80&w=1600" alt="Customer Growth" class="cs-hero-img" loading="eager"/>
  </div></div>
  <div class="cs-body"><div class="container">
    <div class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-section-label">Business Challenge</div>
      <h2>Revenue growth masking a profitability problem.</h2>
      <p>This illustrative case describes a situation common in service businesses. A professional services firm was growing revenue consistently but seeing operating margin under pressure. Leadership's working hypothesis was that cost inflation was the primary driver. The data told a more nuanced story.</p>
      <p>The firm's pricing was largely based on historical precedent and competitive benchmarking, with limited analysis of the actual cost to serve individual clients. Some clients required significantly more senior attention, revision cycles, and operational support than others, but this was not reflected in their pricing.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-section-label">Situation &amp; Context</div>
      <h2>The invisible cross-subsidy problem.</h2>
      <p>What became apparent in the early diagnostic phase was that the firm's revenue-based reporting masked a significant cross-subsidy: a subset of clients were systematically unprofitable on a fully-loaded cost basis, and their losses were being offset by a different subset of highly profitable clients who were, in effect, subsidizing the unprofitable accounts.</p>
      <div class="cs-quote"><p>"We thought we had a cost problem. The data showed we had a pricing problem. Specifically, we were systematically underpricing our most demanding clients."</p></div>
      <p>This pattern is common in service businesses that do not have robust cost-to-serve analytics. Pricing based on market benchmarks or historical precedent will consistently fail to account for the true variation in delivery cost across the client base.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-section-label">Our Approach</div>
      <h2>Building a unified cost-to-serve model at the account level.</h2>
      <p>The engagement began by mapping every cost category in the business to its cost drivers and then linking those cost drivers to client-level activity data. This required connecting financial cost data from the accounting system with operational activity data from the project management and time-tracking systems.</p>
      <p>The result was a cost allocation model that could calculate a fully-loaded cost-to-serve for each client, accounting for senior time, revision cycles, support activity, account management overhead, and delivery complexity.</p>
      <p>When mapped against client revenue, the analysis revealed a customer profitability distribution with a long negative tail: a significant number of accounts were consuming more cost than they were generating in margin. The concentration of profitable accounts was also more extreme than leadership had expected.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-section-label">Key Insights</div>
      <h2>The profitability structure of the client base.</h2>
      <p><strong>The negative-margin accounts shared characteristics:</strong> High revision frequency, complex stakeholder environments, and long sales cycles. These characteristics were knowable at the point of onboarding but had never been used to inform pricing decisions.</p>
      <p><strong>The most profitable accounts were concentrated:</strong> A relatively small number of accounts generated a disproportionate share of the firm's total margin. These relationships were under-invested in relative to their value.</p>
      <p><strong>Pricing had not kept pace with delivery complexity:</strong> For several client segments, the cost to deliver had increased significantly over time while pricing had remained static through annual renewals.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-section-label">Solution</div>
      <h2>A commercial strategy built on cost visibility and value-based pricing.</h2>
      <p>The solution involved three coordinated interventions. First, a client segmentation framework that classified the client base by profitability and strategic value, enabling differentiated commercial and service strategies for each segment.</p>
      <p>Second, a repricing program for the accounts where pricing was systematically misaligned with cost to serve, structured around a value conversation with clients rather than a simple price increase.</p>
      <p>Third, an onboarding complexity assessment process that identified the cost-to-serve implications of potential new clients at the point of proposal, enabling pricing to reflect actual delivery economics from the outset.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-section-label">Business Impact</div>
      <h2>Improved margin through commercial discipline, not cost reduction.</h2>
      <div class="cs-impact-grid">
        <div class="cs-impact-item"><div class="cs-impact-num">Improved</div><div class="cs-impact-label">Operating margin without reducing headcount or cutting costs</div></div>
        <div class="cs-impact-item"><div class="cs-impact-num">Identified</div><div class="cs-impact-label">Cross-subsidy between client segments previously invisible</div></div>
        <div class="cs-impact-item"><div class="cs-impact-num">Built</div><div class="cs-impact-label">Systematic pricing framework anchored to delivery economics</div></div>
        <div class="cs-impact-item"><div class="cs-impact-num">Better</div><div class="cs-impact-label">Commercial decisions at the point of new client onboarding</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cs-section">
      <div class="cs-section-label">Key Takeaways</div>
      <h2>What this type of engagement demonstrates.</h2>
      <p><strong>Revenue is not profit.</strong> Growing a service business by revenue without understanding the profitability of individual accounts is a path to margin erosion. Cost-to-serve analysis at the account level is a foundational commercial discipline, not a finance exercise.</p>
      <p><strong>Pricing decisions require data, not just benchmarks.</strong> Market benchmarks are a starting point, not an answer. Pricing that reflects the actual cost and value of each client relationship requires analytical infrastructure that most businesses have not built.</p>
      <p><strong>Transparency accelerates commercial decisions.</strong> Once leadership could see the profitability of each client relationship on a common basis, commercial strategy conversations became focused and evidence-based rather than hypothetical. The analysis created alignment around difficult decisions.</p>
    </div>
